\v 27 “\add If\add* Yahweh¦175210 won't¦175207 save you, \add ≈how could I possibly\add* save you?” he \add ≈snapped\add*. “\add Some grain\add* from the threshing¦175215 floor¦175215 or¦175216 \add some wine\add* from the wine-pressing¦175219 \add pit\add*?”
\v 28 Then¦175221 he¦175221 \add ≈asked\add* her¦175223, “\add Okay,\add* \add ≈what's¦175225 your problem\add*?”
\p “This¦175230 woman¦175229 said¦175231 to¦175232 me¦175232, ‘\add ≈Let's have\add* your son and¦175221 we'll eat him¦175237 today, then¦175221 we'll eat my son tomorrow¦175243,’ ” she¦175231 \add ≈answered\add*.
\v 29 “\add ≈So¦175245\add* we boiled my son and ate¦175249 him, and then¦175245 the next day¦175252 I said¦175250, ‘\add ≈Hand over\add* your¦175257 son and we'll eat¦175258 him,’ but¦175245 she'd hidden¦175259 \add *him\add*.”
\p
\v 30 When¦175264 he heard¦175265 \add *that\add*, the king¦175266 (standing up on the wall¦175279) tore¦175271 his¦175274 clothes¦175274, and¦175264 the people¦175281 could see¦175280 \add ≈to their astonishment that he was¦175284 wearing sackcloth¦175283 underneath¦175287\add*,
\v 31 and he¦175289 said¦175289, “May¦175292 God¦175295 do¦175292 that to¦175294 me¦175294 and more, if¦175298 Shafat's¦175305 son¦175303 Elisha¦175302 \add ≈still has his head¦175301 on him by the end of the day¦175307\add*.”
\p
\v 32 \add ≈Meanwhile,\add* Elisha was¦175310 sitting¦175310 \add ≈at home\add*, joined by the \add city\add* elders. \add @The king\add* \add ≈had sent a warrior ahead of him\add*, \add ≈and before¦175318 he'd arrived at the house¦175311\add*, \add @Elisha\add* had told the elders, “\add Well now,\add* \add ≈that son¦175332 of a murderer¦175334 has¦175331 sent an assassin to¦175336 separate me from\add* my¦175339 head¦175339. Listen, when that \add ≈hit-man\add* arrives, shut¦175344 the door¦175345 and \add ≈lean against\add* \add *it¦175347\add*. \add ≈His¦175317 master¦175352 won't¦175349 be far\add* behind¦175353 him.”
\v 33 He¦175355 was still¦175373 talking¦175356 with¦175357 them¦175357, when, \add ≈wow, the warrior was coming¦175360 towards the house\add* \add soon followed by the king who\add* said¦175362, “\add ≈Listen, Yahweh¦175368 caused this disaster so why¦175369 should I trust\add* \add *him\add* any longer?”
\c 7
\p
\v 1 “\add ≈Listen to what Yahweh says\add*,” Elisha¦175377 \add ≈responded¦175376\add*, “Yahweh says that at this time¦175386 tomorrow¦175388, a \add ≈drum\add* of flour¦175391 will be \add ≈sold\add* for a shekel, and¦175393 two¦175393 \add ≈drums\add* of barley¦175394 will be \add ≈sold for\add* for a shekel at the¦175396 \add ≈city\add* gate¦175396.”
\p
\v 2 “\add ≈That's impossible\add*,” \add ≈interjected\add* the captain¦175400 \add ≈at¦175417 the king's¦175403 side\add*. “Yahweh¦175414 \add ≈would have to make¦175415\add* windows¦175416 in¦175417 the sky.”
\p “\add Oh, it'll happen\add*,” the man¦175410 of God¦175411 \add ≈answered¦175399\add*, “\add and you'll see it with your own eyes, but you won't get to eat any of it\add*.”
\rem /s1 The Siege Lifted; The Arameans Flee; The Syrian Army Leaves; Lepers Visit the Enemy Camp
\p
\v 3 \add That day,\add* four¦175430 men¦175431 \add who were isolated due to\add* a skin¦175433 disease¦175433 \add ≈were at the city\add* gate¦175435, and¦175436 they¦175436 \add ≈asked each¦175437 other\add*, “Why¦175441 are¦175442 we \add just\add* sitting¦175443 here¦175444 until¦175445 we \add ≈starve\add*?
\v 4 If we \add ≈go into the city, we'll starve to death¦175476 in¦175455 there¦175457, but\add* if we sit¦175460 here¦175461, we'll die \add anyway\add*. \add ≈Why don't we go into the Aramean army camp¦175468\add*—if they kill us then we'll die, but if they let us live, then we'll live.”
\v 5 \add ≈So¦175479 as it was getting dark\add*, they went¦175486 to go¦175481 into the camp¦175484, \add ≈but¦175479 as\add* they came¦175486 to the edge¦175489 of \add *it\add*, \add ≈to their surprise there wasn't a person in¦175480 sight\add*.
\v 6 \add It turned out that\add* the master¦175498 had¦175499 caused¦175499 the Aramean \add ≈army\add* to hear¦175499 the sounds of chariots¦175506 and horses¦175508 and a large¦175511 army, \add ≈so\add* they'd said¦175512 \add ≈to each other that the king¦175521 of Yisrael¦175522 must have hired¦175518 the Hittite¦175526 and Egyptian kings¦175529 to join together to fight them\add*.
\v 7 \add ≈As a result,\add* they'd taken off and fled in¦175536 the twilight¦175536. They'd abandoned¦175537 their tents¦175540 and horses¦175543 and donkeys¦175546—leaving the camp¦175547 just¦175548 as¦175548 it¦175550 was¦175550, and fled for their lives¦175554.
\p
\v 8 \add ≈So¦175556\add* those¦175557 \add four\add* \add ≈men with leprosy approached\add* the edge¦175561 of¦175561 the camp¦175562 and went \add ≈into the first\add* tent, and ate¦175568 and drank¦175569. Then they \add ≈took some\add* gold¦175573 and silver¦175572 and clothes¦175574 out, and they went and hid it. Then they returned¦175577 and went into another¦175582 tent, and \add ≈stole some stuff\add* from¦175584 that one, and went and hid that.
\v 9 Then¦175588 they¦175588 said¦175588 \add ≈to each other\add*, “\add ≈We¦175597 shouldn't¦175593 really be doing¦175598 this¦175600 today\add*. It's a day¦175601 of good news¦175603, but¦175588 we've been \add ≈keeping it to ourselves\add*. \add If\add* we wait¦175607 until¦175608 the light¦175610 of the morning¦175611, \add ≈we'll get punished¦175613 for it\add*, \add ≈so\add* let's go¦175616 now and¦175588 inform the king's¦175619 household¦175618.”
\v 10 \add ≈So¦175621\add* they went and called¦175622 out¦175622 to one of¦175625 the city¦175626 gatekeepers¦175625, and they informed them¦175628, “We¦175630 went to the Aramean camp¦175633, but¦175621 listen, there \add ≈wasn't anyone there, or any voices¦175640\add*. The horses¦175645 and donkeys¦175647 were still tethered¦175648, and the tents¦175649 were left just¦175650 as¦175650 they were.”
\p
\v 11 The¦175655 gatekeepers¦175655 called¦175654 out, and¦175656 \add ≈the news was reported to the palace\add*.
\rem /s1 Israel Plunders the Camp
\v 12 \add ≈Although it¦175661 was already night-time¦175663,\add* the king¦175662 got up and \add ≈warned\add* his¦175667 servants¦175667, “\add ≈I'll tell¦175668 you what's going on¦175688 here\add*: The Arameans¦175677 know¦175678 that we're hungry¦175681, \add ≈so\add* they left their camp¦175686 to¦175676 go and hide¦175687 in¦175688 the countryside¦175688, \add ≈thinking that we'd go out¦175693 from¦175694 the city, and then¦175661 they could capture us and take over\add* the city.”
\p
\v 13 \add ≈But¦175704\add* one¦175705 of his¦175706 servants¦175706 answered¦175704, “\add Sir,\add* please¦175710, let¦175708 us take¦175708 five¦175711 of the remaining horses¦175714. \add ≈They're pretty much finished anyway, like¦175731 the remaining\add* population¦175733 of Yisrael¦175725, \add so there's not much to lose\add*. Let¦175738 us \add ≈go and¦175704 investigate\add*.”
\v 14 \add ≈So¦175741 with the king's¦175746 permission,\add* they¦175741 took¦175741 two¦175742 chariot¦175743 horses¦175744 and¦175741 \add ≈went¦175752 to check out\add* the Aramean¦175750 camp¦175748.
\v 15 They¦175760 went¦175755 \add ≈as far as\add* the Yordan¦175759 \add river\add*, and \add ≈were amazed that\add* the whole¦175761 road¦175763 was¦175764 full¦175764 of clothing¦175765 and equipment¦175766 that the Arameans¦175770 had¦175769 thrown¦175769 off in¦175771 their¦175771 rush, and the messengers¦175774 returned¦175773 and informed the king¦175776.
\v 16 \add ≈So¦175778\add* the people¦175779 went¦175778 out and plundered¦175780 the camp¦175782. Then¦175778 it happened: a \add ≈drum\add* of fine¦175787 flour¦175787 was¦175784 \add ≈sold\add* for a shekel¦175788, and two¦175789 \add ≈drums\add* of barley¦175790 \add ≈for the same amount, just as Yahweh¦175793 had said would happen\add*.
\p
\v 17 The¦175795 king had appointed¦175796 the captain¦175799 who \add ≈had argued with¦175811 Elisha to be in¦175811 charge at¦175811\add* the \add city\add* gate¦175808, but¦175795 the people¦175810 trampled¦175809 him¦175809 \add *there\add* and¦175795 he¦175812 died¦175812, just¦175813 as¦175813 the man¦175815 of¦175815 God¦175816 had said when¦175819 the king \add ≈had gone to his place\add*.
\v 18 When¦175842 the man¦175825 of God¦175826 had¦175824 told the king¦175829 that two¦175831 \add ≈drums\add* of barley¦175832 \add ≈would be sold\add* for a shekel, and¦175834 a \add ≈drum\add* of flour¦175836 \add ≈also sold for\add* a shekel the next morning at¦175833 the \add *city\add* gate¦175841,
